# Day 47/50 — Version Control (Git)

Imagine working on a project for months.

You make changes, experiment with new ideas, remove old features and sometimes wish you could return to an earlier version.

That's where **Version Control** helps.

Tools like Git track changes in files, allowing developers to understand project history, compare versions and collaborate without losing previous work.
Every commit records a snapshot of changes.
You can experiment, create branches and return to earlier versions when needed.

And there's something interesting about that.

Moving forward doesn't mean every previous version becomes useless. Sometimes, looking back helps us understand how far we've come.

**Because progress isn't about having a perfect first version. It's about making every version a little better.**
